# wasmtime-loopback.patch — the cross-tenant wall.
#
# THE HOLE. Tenants share ONE network namespace inside the CVM: every deployment
# is its own wasmtime process listening on 127.0.0.1:<its port>. The egress
# shim's carve-out (a literal loopback IP dials DIRECT, never through the front)
# is what keeps the enclave's own planes reachable — /encvol behind
# ENCLAVE_ENC_API, and the SOCKS front itself for a phase-1 guest — but it is
# unqualified, so it also lets a guest dial a SIBLING TENANT's port and land
# inside its app. That walks around the supervisor's /x/:id, which is where a
# PRIVATE deployment's owner-token check and the deployer's bought WAF live.
# Nothing at the services can close it: another tenant's app is not the
# platform's to gate. In `run` mode it is worse still — a port-serving app is
# granted `-Sinherit-network`, which installs an allow-ALL address check.
#
# THE FIX. `-S loopback-allow=<port[+port...]>` names the loopback ports a
# process may dial; everything else on loopback is refused. Enforced at all
# three places a dial can leave a guest:
#   1. egress.rs connect_addr      — wasi:sockets under transparent egress
#      (that path REPLACES the host connect, so the address check never sees it)
#   2. egress.rs connect_authority — wasi:http literal-IP destinations
#      + connect_direct, the same rule when egress is OFF (wasi:http does not
#      consult the address check at all, so this path would escape it entirely).
#      It judges RESOLVED addresses, so a NAME pointing at loopback is caught.
#   3. src/common.rs socket_addr_check — wasi:sockets with no egress. Installed
#      ONLY where it can TIGHTEN: with egress on (replacing that check with a
#      stricter one) or with inherit-network on (replacing allow-all). With
#      neither, the default is deny-all and we leave it alone.
# Bind is untouched — a tenant must bind its own assigned ports (the manager's
# bind audit polices which). UDP egress stays denied under transparent egress.
#
# UNSET = the previous behaviour (any loopback port), so a hand-launched
# wasmtime — the manager's own probe processes — is unchanged. wasm_manager.py
# passes the flag on EVERY tenant launch, with that deployment's own ports plus
# the manager port (only with encrypted volumes) and the egress front (only when
# one was given); test/wasm-loopback-flag.test.mjs pins that, and
# test/egress.test.mjs drives this patch through a real binary against a real
# listener standing in for the neighbour.
#
# PLUS-SEPARATED, and that is not cosmetic. `-S` splits its OWN argument on
# commas before any option sees the value, so the comma-joined list this patch
# first shipped with parsed as `loopback-allow=<first>` plus a bogus option named
# `<second>`, and wasmtime exited with "unknown -S / --wasi option: <port>".
# Every tenant on the box died at launch the first time an image carrying this
# patch reached the fleet (2026-07-30 00:43Z, release v0.5.296: three
# deployments, all `failed`, card idle, provisioning backing off). The launcher
# greps this option's HELP TEXT for the `+` form to decide whether a toolchain
# speaks it, so a manager that joins with `+` refuses to pass the flag at all to
# an older comma-only binary instead of killing its tenants. The parser still
# accepts ',' for non-CLI callers, where nothing splits it.
#
# Verified against wasmtime v45.0.0 with the full shipped patch stack applied in
# Dockerfile order: cargo check clean, and 18/18 of test/egress.test.mjs green
# on a debug build of the result.
